        {
          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"stendhal-le-rouge-et-le-noir-03b3f49c1d307939\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.93,\"analysis\":\"Amanda directly presents her present affection for Julien as true. The utterance is also strategically used to make him leave the dangerous situation, but that purpose does not turn it into a relational undertaking. Her agitation, blush, repetition, and low voice indicate emotional delivery without independently establishing expressive/reflexive force.\",\"ambiguity\":\"French « je vous aime bien » may express fondness rather than full romantic love, and the avowal is used as leverage; neither qualification changes its predominantly truth-conditional force.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Amanda directly addresses Julien in the café, saying « et cependant, je vous aime bien » while urging him to leave.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Amanda has just come to prefer Julien after witnessing his courage, but she fears a confrontation with another lover. She couples a threat to withdraw affection with an affirmative avowal, using that affection to persuade Julien to leave. The immediate danger and strategic pressure contextualize the statement without displacing its ordinary avowal of fondness.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"« Sortez à l'instant du café, ou je ne vous aime plus; et cependant, je vous aime bien. »\",\"supports\":\"The adversative affirmative clause explicitly presents Amanda's current affection as true, strongly supporting T; its placement also shows a persuasive contextual purpose.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"« Amanda vit son courage [...] en un instant, elle le préféra au grand jeune homme en redingote. »\",\"supports\":\"The narration independently grounds Amanda's newly favorable feeling toward Julien, supporting a sincere or at least narratively grounded avowal.\",\"confidence\":0.96},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Amanda is described as « aussi agitée que lui et fort rouge » and repeats the words « à voix basse » while slowly returning his change.\",\"supports\":\"These details establish emotional intensity and tactical delay, but provide no positive indication that the words escape as a reflex-like discharge; they therefore do not independently support E.\",\"confidence\":0.94},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Amanda seizes Julien by the arm and repeatedly tells him to leave as he moves toward the billiard table and the apparent rival.\",\"supports\":\"The avowal serves the practical aim of preventing a confrontation, explaining its strategic use without establishing performative commitment force.\",\"confidence\":0.97}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures the core avowal of present affection. Its attenuated wording, persuasive use, and emotional delivery are adequately handled as contextual qualifications rather than as P, E, or O.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":\"The negative conditional « je ne vous aime plus » frames the highlighted affirmative avowal but is not itself the primary classified occurrence.\"}"
        }
      ],
